  "summary": "The 'pipeline recipes' / composable blocks approach from round 3 is the strongest proposal so far, but it has a critical blind spot: it assumes the three blocks (plan, spec, impl) are cleanly separable, when in reality the code reveals tight coupling between them that block-level composition doesn't fully address. Specifically: (1) The planning block's exit conditions are entangled with downstream assumptions — _run_pipeline_impl moves items to 'approved' after plan review passes (line 1437-1440), but 'approved' is semantically the entry point for spec-writing. In a plan-only board, what's the terminal stage? 'approved'? 'done'? You'd need a per-recipe stage mapping, not just block selection. (2) The proposal says 'empty directories are harmless' but ignores that the TUI and WebUI actively display and allow interaction with all STAGES — a plan-only board would still show 'implementing', 'testing', etc. as empty columns/tabs, confusing users. The UI layer (tui.py lines 505, 1018-1049 reference STAGES directly) needs board-aware stage filtering, which is a larger change than suggested. (3) The real first step should be even simpler than recipes: add a 'terminal_stage' field per board (e.g., terminal_stage: 'approved' for plan-only). The pipeline already has natural exit points where it checks current_stage before proceeding to the next block. A single config field that says 'stop here and move to done' would cover the primary use case (plan-only boards) with minimal code changes — just one guard clause at the top of run_spec_writing and _run_impl_test_review_loop. Recipes can come later when there are actual use cases for more exotic compositions beyond 'stop after phase X'."
